  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nMarine layer low clouds are redeveloping over the coastal areas \nand western valleys this evening, elsewhere skies are clear. Low \nclouds will continue to spread around 15-20 mi inland through \nsunrise, clearing to the coast by mid morning. Water vapor imagery\nshows a broad upper level high across the southern US centered\nover Texas. This high slowly expands westward through Wednesday \nbefore shifting back eastward and amplifying on Thursday. This \nwill produce a gradual warming trend through the middle of the \nweek along with a shallower marine layer. Areas of night and \nmorning low clouds and fog will generally be confined to the \ncoastal areas and far western valleys Monday night through much of\nthe week. With the ridge axis directly overhead, the subsidence \ninversion and mid-level drying will prevent any deep convection \nfrom developing. There could be a few shallow cumulus clouds over \nthe mountains each afternoon through Wednesday, but no \nprecipitation is forecast.\n\nIn terms of temperatures, highs today will be near to a couple\ndegrees above normal - upper 70s for the coastal areas, mid 80s to\nlow 90s for the western valleys, mid to upper 90s in the inland\nvalleys, mid 70s to upper 80s in the mountains, mid 90s to low\n100s in the high desert, and 105-110 in the low desert.\nTemperatures increase to around 3 to 7 degrees above normal by\nThursday, which is still forecast to be the hottest day of the\nweek for the mountains westward. Heat in the low deserts will \nlikely peak earlier in the week as a return of monsoonal moisture \non Thursday may limit heating there. By Wednesday, there is a\n90-100% chance of at least moderate HeatRisk over the inland\nvalleys, lower elevation mountains, and deserts. Chances of major\nHeatRisk are around 10-30% over the far inland valleys, around 50%\nfor the lower elevation pars of the high desert, and 55-85% for\nthe low desert. While high temperatures won't be significantly\nabove normal, continued humidity and above normal overnight\ntemperatures will contribute to the higher HeatRisk. These\nprobabilities increase slightly on Thursday. Only a couple degrees\nof cooling is expected each day Friday through the weekend as the\nupper high shifts back eastward.\n\nAs the high becomes centered closer to the Four Corners region\nlate in the week, south to southeasterly flow aloft will help\nbring in another round of monsoonal moisture. Moisture begins to\nincrease by Thursday, but with the ridge still overhead\ninstability is lacking, so precipitation chances over the\nmountains is only 10-15%. Chances increase to around 15-20% each\nafternoon for the mountains and parts of the deserts Friday\nthrough Monday.",
